WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:13.482
Marie Curie is one of the most famous scientists who ever lived.

2
00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:17.541
A brilliant physicist, she was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize,

3
00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:23.598
and the only woman to win Nobel Prizes in two different fields—physics and chemistry.

4
00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:30.439
Marie was born Maria Sklodowska on November 7, 1867, in Warsaw, Poland.

5
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:38.621
Education was very important in her family, and little Manya, as her parents called her, had an amazing memory.

6
00:00:38.622 --> 00:00:46.437
Her father was a math and physics teacher who often showed scientific equipment to his children and read classics out loud to them.

7
00:00:46.438 --> 00:00:51.834
Manya worked hard at school and graduated early, around the age of 16.

8
00:00:51.835 --> 00:01:00.422
Manya and her sister, Bronya, wanted to continue their studies, but back then, women were not admitted to universities in Poland.

9
00:01:00.423 --> 00:01:04.126
For awhile they participated in a "floating university,"

10
00:01:04.127 --> 00:01:11.252
a secret organization that met in different locations each night to provide education and discuss politics.

11
00:01:11.253 --> 00:01:16.954
Eventually Manya and Bronya decided to go to Sorbonne University in Paris, France.

12
00:01:16.955 --> 00:01:21.036
There was one problem—they were quite poor.

13
00:01:21.037 --> 00:01:31.333
So the sisters made a plan. Bronya went to Paris first, to study medicine, while Manya stayed in Poland to work and help pay for Bronya's education.

14
00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:36.067
Later Bronya could use her earnings to help pay for Manya's education.

15
00:01:36.068 --> 00:01:42.647
Manya worked as a tutor and governess, reading about physics and chemistry in her spare time.

16
00:01:42.648 --> 00:01:48.531
Six years after Bronya left for Paris, she finally sent good news.

17
00:01:48.532 --> 00:01:53.555
Bronya was a doctor now and could help pay for Manya's education.

18
00:01:53.556 --> 00:02:04.461
In November 1891 Manya registered at the Sorbonne using "Marie," the French version of her name. Marie's time there was not easy.

19
00:02:04.462 --> 00:02:09.003
She was always busy with her studies, and she hardly slept.

20
00:02:09.004 --> 00:02:14.770
Sometimes she even forgot to eat! But Marie's hard work paid off.

21
00:02:14.771 --> 00:02:20.941
After three years she earned a master's degree in physics and graduated first in her class.

22
00:02:20.942 --> 00:02:25.999
The following year she earned another degree—this time, in mathematics.

23
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.787
In 1894 Marie was working on a research project and needed a laboratory to conduct scientific tests.

24
00:02:33.788 --> 00:02:40.202
A friend told her about Pierre Curie, a young physicist who had a large laboratory to share.

25
00:02:40.203 --> 00:02:46.191
Smart, intense, and passionate about physics, he was a perfect match for Marie.

26
00:02:46.192 --> 00:02:56.154
They were married in July 1895. Pierre was working on his doctorate in physics and taught part-time, so the couple did not have much money.

27
00:02:56.155 --> 00:03:01.895
For their honeymoon they bought two bicycles and then rode around the village where they'd gotten married!

28
00:03:01.896 --> 00:03:05.676
Marie knew she wanted to earn a doctorate degree.

29
00:03:05.677 --> 00:03:09.995
Until then no woman in France had ever earned a doctorate.

30
00:03:09.996 --> 00:03:14.994
First Marie needed to decide upon a research topic for her degree.

31
00:03:14.995 --> 00:03:21.068
She eventually decided to continue the work of a French scientist named Henri Becquerel.

32
00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:27.474
A physicist, Becquerel had found that rocks containing uranium give off light, or rays.

33
00:03:27.475 --> 00:03:33.201
Marie was interested in this unusual phenomenon, and she wanted to learn more about it.

34
00:03:33.202 --> 00:03:40.849
Marie's experiments led her to the idea that this phenomenon comes from changes in the atoms that make up the rocks.

35
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:52.283
Her discovery was a new idea—until then scientists had believed that atoms could not change their form. Marie called the phenomenon "radioactivity."

36
00:03:52.284 --> 00:03:56.237
Pierre eventually became immersed in her research too.

37
00:03:56.238 --> 00:04:00.211
They investigated whether other matter had radioactivity.

38
00:04:00.212 --> 00:04:04.290
In the course of their work, the couple discovered two new elements:

39
00:04:04.291 --> 00:04:12.426
polonium, which Marie named after her beloved Poland, and radium, named after the Latin word for rays.

40
00:04:12.427 --> 00:04:19.594
The scientists worked hard, and often in poor conditions, as their laboratory was not properly insulated.

41
00:04:19.595 --> 00:04:29.126
They frequently felt sick and tired. The Curies did not know that the radioactive rays were actually very bad for their health.

42
00:04:29.127 --> 00:04:33.793
Exposure to radioactive materials can cause many illnesses.

43
00:04:33.794 --> 00:04:40.079
In 1903 Marie published her discoveries on radioactivity with great success.

44
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:47.780
She also learned that she and Pierre were being given one of the highest awards in scienceㅡthe Nobel Prize for Physics.

45
00:04:47.781 --> 00:04:51.076
They shared this prize with Henri Becquerel.

46
00:04:51.077 --> 00:04:57.238
On April 19, 1906, the Curies' partnership ended tragically.

47
00:04:57.239 --> 00:05:04.341
While crossing the street, Pierre was run over by a horse-drawn carriage, and died instantly.

48
00:05:04.342 --> 00:05:09.832
Marie was devastated, but she believed she had to carry on with her work.

49
00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:16.071
She started teaching at the Sorbonne, becoming the university's first female professor.

50
00:05:16.072 --> 00:05:20.765
And she began researching ways to use radium to benefit others.

51
00:05:20.766 --> 00:05:28.553
During World War I Marie focused on expanding the use of x-ray machines to diagnose medical conditions.

52
00:05:28.554 --> 00:05:36.067
She trained nurses to use them, and with the help of the French government, she also created "little Curies."

53
00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:42.545
These were portable x-ray machines that could be used to examine wounded soldiers immediately, on the battlefield.

54
00:05:42.546 --> 00:05:46.511
After the war ended, Marie's health worsened.

55
00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:52.898
Exposure to radioactive materials was increasingly damaging her health, but she kept working.

56
00:05:52.899 --> 00:06:02.692
She even received another Nobel Prize, this time for chemistry, becoming the first person—and only woman—to receive two Nobel Prizes.

57
00:06:02.693 --> 00:06:09.370
Her health never improved, and Marie died in 1934 at the age of 66.

58
00:06:09.371 --> 00:06:13.537
Marie Curie's discoveries helped to create many of today's technologies,

59
00:06:13.538 --> 00:06:19.344
such as the use of radioactivity to treat cancer, make electricity, and produce heat.

60
00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:31.120
Her work has greatly influenced the way we live, and it paved the way for other scientists to make other discoveries.
